# 40
#title Find the VS Code command in the usual Linux install locations
#url https://github.com/MobileReality/lantern/pull/40
#branch feat/linux-code-lookup
#base main
#opened 2026-11-12
#head 2c821e588d9f575dd4761c82f44d484c78169fc9
#describe what The embedded editor looks for the VS Code command in the usual Linux install locations and on PATH, so a Linux install is launched directly instead of falling back to the serve-web download.
#describe before On Linux only /usr/local/bin/code and the macOS paths were tried.
#describe after /usr/bin, snap, /usr/share/code, /opt/visual-studio-code, ~/.local/bin, the flatpak exports and PATH are tried too; macOS and Windows are unchanged.
#describe verified typecheck and lint green; pnpm test green except an unrelated self-test that times out under vitest and passes when run directly.
+ src/main/serveweb-code-paths.ts
+ test/serveweb-code-paths.test.ts
~ src/main/serveweb.ts
#order src/main/serveweb-code-paths.ts
#order src/main/serveweb.ts
#order test/serveweb-code-paths.test.ts
#reason src/main/serveweb-code-paths.ts Linux installs put code in many places, so the lookup is a pure, injectable function that tests can cover without real files.
#reason test/serveweb-code-paths.test.ts Pins that each Linux location is found, the order is fixed and the Linux paths stay out of macOS and Windows.
#reason src/main/serveweb.ts The inline macOS-only candidate list could not find a deb install, so it now calls the shared lookup.
#story Linux users get the installed editor
#step actor A Linux user
#step action opens the embedded editor
#file src/main/serveweb.ts
#step subject a VS Code installed by deb, rpm, snap or flatpak
#file src/main/serveweb-code-paths.ts
#below interaction The installed code command is used directly
#file test/serveweb-code-paths.test.ts
#below consequence Only macOS paths were tried, so a download followed => The usual Linux locations are tried first
